Round 4 review of a static stack-depth checker for a compiler's test gate (scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py in the Concrete repository, slice 0): it reads the disassembly of a small linked test executable and must report an upper bound on how deep the program's own code uses the stack, or refuse the input. The diff ./DELTA.diff (9ed1e1cff..HEAD) adapts it to macOS AArch64 executables: (a) calls to imported library functions go through Mach-O stub entries, identified only by exact addresses read from the indirect symbol table (`stub ADDR SYMBOL` lines the gate appends); since round 3, a call into the middle of a stub entry or to the stub section without an entry is refused (controls added); (b) single-register saves and restores of x30/x29 (`str x30, [sp, #k]` / `ldr x30, [sp, #k]`) are modelled as saved slots, readable only by the matching restore, and all stack reads also require bytes the function itself wrote since its last call; (c) the link-register validity flag is combined by conjunction at control-flow joins. Round 3 results: one finding fixed (the stub fallback by section name); a second reviewer's reproducer (a saved x29 slot reread after a call) is refused by the written-bytes rule ("reads stack bytes this function has not written"). CI: all jobs green on Linux x86-64 and macOS AArch64; real macOS disassemblies are attached (answer.lcb.aarch64-macos.dis, answer.llvm.aarch64-macos.dis).
